00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:32.960
hurt and that was bothering you.
疼痛或不舒服的部位。

314
00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:38.420
And so he diagnosed me with an ear infection or at least inner ear congestion.
他诊断我得了耳部感染，或者至少是内耳充血。

315
00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:44.300
And he ended up putting some oil from the kitchen that we found in my ear.
最后他从厨房找了点油滴进我耳朵里。

316
00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:46.600
It turns out you can do this.
事实证明这招确实可行。

317
00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:50.740
It is a home remedy.
这算是一种家庭疗法。

318
00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:51.780
And it helped.
而且还挺管用。

319
00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:52.640
It helped my ear.
我的耳朵好多了。

320
00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:55.160
And I think we diagnosed him with walking pneumonia.
我想我们当时诊断他得了行走性肺炎。

321
00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:00.060
So we did this right when we were sent off to write the Beach Games episode.
我们就是在被派去写《海滩游戏》那一集的时候干的这些事。

322
00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:05.860
And it ended up becoming our cold open because we were probably looking for a cold open.
最后这成了我们的开场戏，因为我们当时可能正愁没开场戏写。

323
00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:08.920
And we're like, what about what we're doing right now?
我们当时就想，不如就把我们现在做的事写进去？

324
00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:16.640
There is so much about that audio clip that I love.
那段音频里有很多我喜欢的内容。

325
00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:18.160
It's so relatable.
太有共鸣了。

326
00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:21.980
It's like whenever you sit down to do something, and it doesn't matter what it is.
就像你每次坐下来准备做点什么的时候，不管是什么事。

327
00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:26.100
I mean, theirs was this writing assignment, but all the different ways you procrastinate
我是说，他们当时是写作任务，但在真正开始工作之前，

328
00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:27.780
before you get to that thing.
你会有各种各样的拖延方式。

329
00:18:28.420 --> 00:18:28.680
Yes.
没错。

330
00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:33.340
Well, Angela, you and I are working on a project that we can't say exactly what it is, but it
安吉拉，你和我正在做一个项目，虽然不能透露具体是什么，但

331
00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:34.940
involves quite a bit of writing.
涉及大量的写作工作。

332
00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:43.580
And every time I sit down to write, I will write one sentence, and then I will get up
每次我坐下来写作，写上一句，就会站起来

333
00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:44.580
and get a snack.
去吃点零食。

334
00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:48.020
And then I'll come back and write a sentence, and then I'll be like, I need more coffee.
回来再写一句，然后又会想，我得再去冲杯咖啡。

335
00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:51.380
Yeah, it's like the procrastination.
是啊，这就是拖延症。

336
00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:58.260
I loved that she shared that because I think my perception is always that creativity comes
我很高兴她分享了这些，因为我总觉得成功人士的创作过程

337
00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:00.600
easy to people who are successful.
一定很轻松。

338
00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:03.020
I always think like it must pour out of them.
我总觉得灵感一定是从他们脑子里源源不断地涌出来的。

339
00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:05.200
And it is such a slog.
但实际上这过程非常艰辛。

340
00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:06.800
So I loved hearing that.
所以我很喜欢听她这么说。

341
00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:14.200
But then the other thing I loved was that their procrastination, it got turned into a story
但我还喜欢一点，就是他们的拖延行为被转化成了故事素材，

342
00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:20.460
idea, like it's worth it, like all of that nonsense, it produced something, right?
这很值得，那些胡闹的行为最终产出了成果，对吧？

343
00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:24.920
Well, I had a writing professor in college that used to always say, write what you know,
我在大学时有一位写作教授，他总是说，写你所知道的，

344
00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:27.120
write what you know, and it will be honeSt.
写你所知道的，这样才会真实。

345
00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.900
Oh, well, I just loved this, this opening.
噢，我太喜欢这个开场了。

346
00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:39.080
And, you know, before they can really diagnose Michael, Pam walks in the office and says, David
而且，在他们还没来得及给迈克尔诊断完时，帕姆走进办公室说，大卫·华莱士

